  "answer": "The study found none of the 5 cases it was looking for, and no timing or speed of the lead car made VaVAM rear-end it at 2 Hz planning. Only two runs failed, at S2 (023b7fcc, lead car at 0.75x speed) and S11 (0cf6368d, 0.5 s earlier). Both were side or front contact with a car in the next lane after the ego drifted out of its own lane. The ego did not run into the car ahead, but the policy was at fault both times. At those two settings the failure rate could be anywhere from low to high, and every other setting had too few runs to tell much. The search barely tried the brief's range: only one setting slowed the lead car, none sped it up, and nothing was shifted by more than 1 s. So the study does not rule out rear-ends at the edges of the range.",
